# Role 2 — Stockist

## Overview

A Stockist is a licensed pharmaceutical distributor supplying medicines and healthcare products to pharmacies.

The stockist is the seller within the B2B relationship.

The stockist manages product availability, inventory, invoicing, fulfilment, collections, and delivery operations.

---

## Primary Goals

A stockist wants to:

- Build pharmacy relationships.
- Sell products.
- Process incoming orders.
- Maintain inventory.
- Generate invoices.
- Dispatch deliveries.
- Receive payments.
- Process returns.
- Monitor business performance.

---

## Responsibilities

A stockist is responsible for:

- Maintaining valid licenses.
- Maintaining inventory.
- Maintaining product pricing.
- Managing batches.
- Managing expiry.
- Accepting or rejecting orders.
- Packing orders.
- Dispatching deliveries.
- Managing collections.
- Processing returns.
- Issuing credit notes.
- Managing delivery staff.
- Managing business users.

---

## Business Ownership

A stockist owns:

- Business profile.
- Product catalogue.
- Inventory.
- Batch information.
- Pricing.
- Pharmacy relationships.
- Orders.
- Invoices.
- Delivery operations.
- Financial records.
- Staff.
- Business documents.

---

## Relationships

A stockist may trade with many pharmacies simultaneously.

The stockist controls whether a pharmacy becomes an approved trading partner.

---

## Permissions

A stockist should be able to:

- Register.
- Complete verification.
- Manage profile.
- Manage staff.
- Manage pharmacy connections.
- View incoming orders.
- Accept orders.
- Reject orders.
- Edit pending orders.
- Allocate inventory.
- Pack orders.
- Generate invoices.
- Dispatch deliveries.
- Mark deliveries complete.
- Record inventory movements.
- Receive payments.
- Approve payments.
- Reject payments.
- Place payments on hold.
- Approve returns.
- Reject returns.
- Issue credit notes.
- Manage delivery routes.
- View analytics.
- Export reports.

---

## Restrictions

A stockist should not be able to:

- Modify completed financial records.
- Edit locked invoices.
- Edit delivered orders.
- Access another stockist's data.
- View another stockist's inventory.
- Bypass verification.
- Remove audit history.

---

## Success

A stockist successfully completes its business cycle when it can:

Receive Order

↓

Accept

↓

Pack

↓

Invoice

↓

Dispatch

↓

Deliver

↓

Receive Payment

↓

Complete Settlement

without requiring manual tracking.

